# A bonus scene: meta files, small files the story puts beside the game, where the player
# can find them. main.vns includes this file and calls hana_file near the start of the
# story, so every new game writes the files again.
#
# The files go in the characters folder of the game folder, and nowhere else. Players
# can open, move, or delete them, and the story can check what they did. Every rule is in
# Help ▸ Guides ▸ Script language, "Meta files".

label hana_file:
    # "file write" puts a file in the characters folder. With only a name, it copies the
    # file of that name from the files folder: files/hana.chr, here a picture of Hana saved
    # under another name. The original in files/ never changes. A write can't be undone, so
    # the player can't roll back past it.
    file write "hana.chr"
    # With text after the name, file write writes that text into the file instead. \n
    # starts a new line in the file.
    file write "read_me.txt" "If you're reading this, don't tell Hana.\nShe can hear you."
    hana "Oh, and I left something for you, in your game folder. Look in the characters folder."
    # Text effects move the words between a tag and its closing tag while the line is up.
    # {wave} makes them ripple up and down; {shake} and {glitch} come below. Each takes
    # small, medium (the default), or large, e.g. {wave=large}. Players who turn on Reduce
    # motion and flashing in Settings see them hold still, so, as with shake and flash,
    # never let an effect carry the story on its own. Every rule is in
    # Help ▸ Guides ▸ Script language, "Text effects".
    hana happy "That's me in there. hana.chr. Don't open it, it's {wave}private{/wave}."
    # "file delete" deletes a file the story wrote. Like a write, it can't be undone. The
    # original in files/ stays, so a new game writes the file again.
    file delete "hana.chr"
    # "ui style" switches the text box to a style from config.toml, here [style.dark],
    # while Hana's file is gone. The box's colors change at once and its text with the
    # next line. "ui style off" brings back the usual look; saves keep the style. Every
    # rule is in Help ▸ Guides ▸ Script language, "Dynamic UI".
    ui style dark
    # Glitch effects: the picture seems to break while Hana's file is gone. The lines say
    # what happens too, so players who turn effects down miss nothing. Every rule is in
    # Help ▸ Guides ▸ Script language, "Glitch effects".
    # "with glitch" is a transition: the old picture tears, cuts to the new one, and
    # settles.
    show hana surprised with glitch
    # "effect" glitches the stage picture; the text box never glitches. "effect jitter"
    # and a sprite's tag make that sprite twitch. A length in seconds makes a short burst.
    effect jitter hana 0.8
    # {shake} makes the words tremble; here, small, while the sprite twitches too.
    hana surprised "...Huh. Did I just {shake=small}disappear{/shake} from your folder?"
    # "on" keeps a glitch going until an "off". "effect tear" slides thin bands of the
    # picture sideways; small keeps it gentle.
    effect tear small on
    # {glitch} flickers some letters into symbols such as # and %. Keep it short: History
    # always shows the real words.
    hana "There's a copy of me in the files folder. You could {glitch}put me back{/glitch}, if you like."
    # "off" after a glitch's name stops that one glitch.
    effect tear off
    # "effect colorshift" pulls the picture's red and blue apart.
    effect colorshift small on
    # "effect static" lays gray noise over the picture. Glitches that start on the same
    # line count once toward the game's limit of 3 flashes a second.
    effect static small on
    thought "I wasn't sure what she meant. I let her go on anyway."
    # "file exists" sets a story variable to True when the file is in the characters
    # folder, and to False when it isn't. hana_here has a default in main.vns.
    file exists "hana.chr" into hana_here
    # "effect off" stops every glitch at once.
    effect off
    ui style off
    if hana_here:
        hana happy "You put me back! I knew you would."
    else:
        hana "Never mind. A new game always brings me back."
    # Fourth-wall tricks: for a moment the game seems to reach outside its window. Name
    # them in your content warning (config.toml's [content_warning] shows how). Every rule
    # is in Help ▸ Guides ▸ Script language, "Fourth-wall tricks".
    # "effect window_title" changes the words in the window's title bar. A save keeps it,
    # so loading one brings it back.
    effect window_title "Hana is still here"
    # {username} shows the player's computer user name, but only because config.toml has
    # [meta] username = true and only if the player chose "Allow" when the game asked.
    # Otherwise it shows "friend". History, saves, and save pictures never hold the name.
    hana "I can see more from in here than you think, {username}."
    # "effect crash" shows a fake error screen, as if the game had broken. Nothing on it
    # moves; Esc, Enter, or a click continues the story, and closing the window quits at
    # once. The message may use {username}. Never copy a real system error screen or add
    # a phone number to call.
    effect crash "HanaError: Hana is not responding.\nShe is still here, {username}."
    hana surprised "...Sorry. That wasn't me. Was it?"
    # "effect window_title off" brings back the game's own title, from config.toml.
    effect window_title off
    return
